@broadcast "DOOMCAST — Seven-Day Outlook"
@channel wx9
@category weather
@host npc_dex_rime
@length 120
@type weather
@titlecard wx_doomcast_title

::actors
@actor npc_dex_rime
@alias npc_dex_rime DEX
::endactors

::asset wx_doomcast_title
<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 640 360" font-family="'Segoe UI',Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif">
  <defs>
    <linearGradient id="wxsky" x1="0" y1="0" x2="0" y2="1">
      <stop offset="0" stop-color="#0a1524"/>
      <stop offset="0.55" stop-color="#0a1018"/>
      <stop offset="1" stop-color="#05070c"/>
    </linearGradient>
    <radialGradient id="wxsweep" cx="0.5" cy="0.5" r="0.5">
      <stop offset="0" stop-color="#35e0d6" stop-opacity="0.9"/>
      <stop offset="1" stop-color="#35e0d6" stop-opacity="0"/>
    </radialGradient>
  </defs>

  <rect width="640" height="360" fill="url(#wxsky)"/>

  <!-- scanlines -->
  <g stroke="#0f2030" stroke-width="1" opacity="0.35">
    <line x1="0" y1="60" x2="640" y2="60"/>
    <line x1="0" y1="120" x2="640" y2="120"/>
    <line x1="0" y1="180" x2="640" y2="180"/>
    <line x1="0" y1="240" x2="640" y2="240"/>
    <line x1="0" y1="300" x2="640" y2="300"/>
  </g>

  <!-- radar dish, upper-right -->
  <g transform="translate(500,110)">
    <circle r="78" fill="#07131c" stroke="#173444" stroke-width="1.5"/>
    <circle r="52" fill="none" stroke="#173444" stroke-width="1"/>
    <circle r="26" fill="none" stroke="#173444" stroke-width="1"/>
    <line x1="-78" y1="0" x2="78" y2="0" stroke="#173444" stroke-width="1"/>
    <line x1="0" y1="-78" x2="0" y2="78" stroke="#173444" stroke-width="1"/>
    <g>
      <path d="M0 0 L78 0 A78 78 0 0 0 55 -55 Z" fill="url(#wxsweep)"/>
      <animateTransform attributeName="transform" type="rotate" from="0" to="360" dur="4s" repeatCount="indefinite"/>
    </g>
    <circle cx="34" cy="-20" r="3" fill="#f5a623"/>
    <circle cx="-40" cy="14" r="2.5" fill="#ff4a4a"/>
    <circle cx="10" cy="46" r="2" fill="#35e0d6"/>
  </g>

  <!-- lightning bolt accent, lower-left -->
  <path d="M120 208 L96 262 L118 262 L100 316 L156 250 L132 250 L152 208 Z"
        fill="#f5a623" opacity="0.92"/>

  <!-- title -->
  <text x="40" y="150" fill="#eaf4f8" font-size="76" font-weight="800" letter-spacing="4">DOOMCAST</text>
  <rect x="42" y="168" width="300" height="3" fill="#35e0d6"/>
  <text x="44" y="196" fill="#35e0d6" font-size="19" font-weight="600" letter-spacing="6">WX-9 · METEOWATCH</text>

  <!-- footer band -->
  <rect x="0" y="322" width="640" height="38" fill="#0c1a24"/>
  <rect x="0" y="322" width="640" height="2" fill="#f5a623"/>
  <text x="20" y="347" fill="#9fb8c4" font-size="15" font-weight="600" letter-spacing="3">SEVEN-DAY OUTLOOK</text>
  <text x="620" y="347" fill="#eaf4f8" font-size="15" font-weight="600" letter-spacing="2" text-anchor="end">with DOPPLER DEX RIME</text>
</svg>
::endasset

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# DOOMCAST — line library for the weathercaster "Doppler" Dex Rime.
# The runner assembles a fresh report each airing from the live 7-day forecast,
# picking one line per matching pool and filling {tokens}. Weather forecasts are
# acted live: Dex must be in the studio or the channel drops to camera-idle then
# technical difficulties. See docs/bsm-format.md#weather-broadcasts-type-weather.
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# ── Sign-on (time-of-day preferred, generic fallback) ──────────────────────
::lines intro.morning
Morning, what's left of you. Doppler Dex Rime, DOOMCAST, and the sky's already made its mind up.
Rise and shine — well, rise. This is the {season} outlook, and it owes you nothing.
Up early? Bold. Dex Rime here with the forecast the Bureau doesn't want narrated.
::endlines

::lines intro.afternoon
Afternoon, survivors. Dex Rime, DOOMCAST, halfway through another {season} the planet's clearly regretting.
You made it to midday. Let's see what the atmosphere's charging you for the rest of it.
::endlines

::lines intro.evening
Evening. Dex Rime with the DOOMCAST, because somebody has to tell you what's coming after dark.
Good evening. The sun's clocking out, and I've got the overnight bad news ready to go.
::endlines

::lines intro.night
Still awake? Same. Dex Rime, DOOMCAST, graveyard edition — here's the sky at this ungodly hour.
Night owls, insomniacs, fugitives: Dex Rime here. The forecast doesn't sleep either.
::endlines

::lines intro
Doppler Dex Rime, and this is the only DOOMCAST that still bothers to check.
Dex Rime here. Let's find out what the atmosphere's planning to do to you.
::endlines

# ── Hand-off into current conditions ───────────────────────────────────────
::lines today.lead
Here's what's waiting the second you step outside.
Current conditions — and I do mean conditions.
Right now, out there, this is the situation.
Let's start with the here, the now, and the regrettable.
::endlines

# ── Sky by weather type (today AND each forecast day) ──────────────────────
::lines sky.clear
Clear skies. Enjoy the exposure while the atmosphere pretends to be your friend.
Not a cloud in it — just wide-open sky and whatever's still falling out of it.
{day}: clear. Suspiciously clear. Trust nothing.
::endlines

::lines sky.fog
Fog. Visibility measured in arm's-lengths. Walk into things carefully out there.
A thick fog's rolled in — the city's gone, and honestly it's an improvement.
{day}: fog, {humidity}% humidity, and the distinct sense you're being followed.
::endlines

::lines sky.haze
Haze hanging over everything — that flat grey soup we call air these days.
A dirty haze today. Breathe shallow, blink often.
{day}: haze. The skyline's a rumour.
::endlines

::lines sky.cloudy
Clouds, plural, doing that oppressive committee thing they do.
Partly cloudy — the sky can't commit, same as everyone.
{day}: cloud cover, {temp} degrees, mood grey.
::endlines

::lines sky.overcast
Full overcast. A lid on the whole city. No sky, just ceiling.
Overcast and staying that way — the sun filed for absence.
{day}: solid overcast. Bring your own light.
::endlines

::lines sky.rain
Rain, {precip}% of it, because of course. Bring something waterproof that isn't your skin.
It's coming down out there. Not clean rain — is it ever — but rain.
{day}: rain at {precip}%. Puddles with opinions.
::endlines

::lines sky.sleet
Sleet. The worst of both worlds, arriving sideways.
Half rain, half ice, all misery — that's sleet, and it's here.
{day}: sleet, {temp} degrees. Dress for betrayal.
::endlines

::lines sky.snow
Snow. Pretty from a window, lethal from a doorway.
It's snowing — {temp} degrees of it settling on everything that stops moving.
{day}: snow. Layer up or become part of the scenery.
::endlines

::lines sky.thunderstorm
Thunderstorm rolling through. If you can count the gap to the thunder, you're too close.
Lightning's on the menu. {precip}% chance of the sky picking a target.
{day}: thunderstorms. Metal roofs, unlucky people — stay clear of both.
::endlines

::lines sky.blizzard
A full blizzard. {temp} degrees, feels like {feels}, feels like the end.
Whiteout conditions — if you can read this, you're indoors, and you should stay that way.
{day}: blizzard. This one kills the unprepared. Don't audition.
::endlines

::lines sky.storm
A proper storm — wind, water, and the power grid holding its breath.
The whole system's dropped on us at once. {wind} km/h and climbing.
{day}: storm-force everything. Tie down what you love.
::endlines

# ── Temperature bands ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
::lines temp.frigid
{temp} degrees. Your extremities are officially on their own out there.
{temp} — that's not weather, that's a warning. Exposed skin freezes fast.
::endlines

::lines temp.cold
A biting {temp} degrees. Every gap in your coat is a mistake you'll feel.
{temp} and cold enough to bite. Keep moving or keep warm; pick one.
::endlines

::lines temp.cool
{temp} degrees, cool and grey. Jacket weather, if you own a jacket.
A cool {temp}. Not cruel, just unwelcoming — the city's default setting.
::endlines

::lines temp.mild
A frankly suspicious {temp} degrees. Enjoy it, distrust it.
{temp} and mild — the atmosphere's clearly planning something. It always is.
::endlines

::lines temp.warm
A warm {temp} degrees. Almost pleasant, if you don't think about why.
{temp} and climbing into comfortable. Savour it before it curdles.
::endlines

::lines temp.hot
{temp} degrees and hot. Hydrate, stay shaded, and don't touch the metal.
A hard {temp}. The pavement's cooking. So are you, slower.
::endlines

::lines temp.scorching
{temp} degrees — scorching. This is heatstroke weather. Treat the sun as hostile.
{temp}. That's not summer, that's an oven with a schedule. Stay inside if you can.
::endlines

# ── Wind (only voiced when notable) ────────────────────────────────────────
::lines wind.calm
Dead calm, too — not a breath of wind, which fog and worse just love.
::endlines

::lines wind.windy
Add a {wind} km/h wind to file the warmth right off you.
And a steady {wind} km/h blow — {windLabel} — so mind the loose signage.
::endlines

::lines wind.strong
Strong winds at {wind} km/h. Anything not bolted down is now airborne inventory.
::endlines

::lines wind.gale
A {wind} km/h gale on top — {windLabel}. Lean into it and pray your boots hold.
::endlines

# ── Humidity (only voiced dry / oppressive) ────────────────────────────────
::lines humid.dry
Bone-dry air at {humidity}% — your lips will crack before your patience does.
::endlines

::lines humid.oppressive
And {humidity}% humidity — the air's basically soup. You won't so much breathe as wade.
::endlines

# ── Severe-weather warnings (day severity ≥ 0.45) ──────────────────────────
::lines warn.cold
⚠ Bundle up {day} — {temp} degrees will stop your heart if the muggers don't.
⚠ Cold warning for {day}. Thermal gear isn't optional; it's the difference.
::endlines

::lines warn.heat
⚠ Heat warning, {day}. {temp} degrees. Shade, water, and don't be a hero at noon.
⚠ {day} bakes at {temp}. Heatstroke's free and quick — decline it.
::endlines

::lines warn.wind
⚠ Wind warning for {day} — {wind} km/h. Stay off the rooftops and away from the glass.
⚠ {day}: gale-force gusts. If it can fall on you, assume it will.
::endlines

::lines warn.blizzard
⚠ Severe: {day} is a killer. Thermal gear or a will — your call.
⚠ Blizzard warning, {day}. {feels}-degree windchill. This one takes stragglers.
::endlines

::lines warn.storm
⚠ Storm warning for {day}. Lightning, wind, and a grid that may not hold. Stay in.
⚠ {day} brings the whole storm. Charge what you can now — power's not promised.
::endlines

::lines warn.generic
⚠ Take {day} seriously — the numbers say severe, and the numbers don't flatter.
::endlines

# ── Hand-off into the week ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
::lines forecast.lead
Looking down the barrel of the week, and I use "looking" generously.
Now the seven-day — brace accordingly.
Here's the outlook, such as it is.
Let's walk the week. Try not to make plans.
::endlines

# ── Forecast-day transitions ───────────────────────────────────────────────
::lines ahead.tomorrow
Tomorrow:
Come tomorrow —
::endlines

::lines ahead.midweek
Midweek, around {day} —
By {day}:
::endlines

::lines ahead.weekend
Come the weekend —
Toward {day}, if we get there:
::endlines

::lines ahead.next
Then {day} —
::endlines

# ── Week-arc trend ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
::lines trend.warming
Trend's upward — {loTemp} today crawling toward {hiTemp} by week's end. Make of that what you will.
It warms as the week goes. {hiTemp} degrees waiting at the far side.
::endlines

::lines trend.cooling
It's all downhill — we shed heat from {hiTemp} to {loTemp} before the week's out. Dig out the layers.
Cooling trend. {loTemp} degrees is where this week is dragging us.
::endlines

::lines trend.clearing
The good news, and I choke saying it — the wet clears as the week runs on.
It dries out toward the weekend. Briefly. Don't get attached.
::endlines

::lines trend.deteriorating
It gets worse. {severeCount} severe days ahead, {worstDay} the worst of them. Plan around it or pay for it.
Deteriorating fast — {severeCount} days flagged severe this week. {worstDay}'s the one to fear.
::endlines

::lines trend.steady
Steady as a flatline the whole week — same grey, same grind. Consistency, at least.
No real change ahead. The sky's found its rut and it's staying in it.
::endlines

# ── Sign-off ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
::lines outro
That's the DOOMCAST. The weather's still trying to kill you — I'm just the one who says so out loud.
Doppler Dex Rime. Stay sealed, stay indoors, stay suspicious of clear skies. Back to you.
That's your seven days. Dress for the worst of them. Rime, out.
::endlines

# ── Ad-libs (format-reserved; the current runner doesn't inject these yet) ──
::lines adlib.grim
...not that any of us are going far.
...for whatever that's worth to a person your age.
::endlines

END
